assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2001
says       A child that sends itself each of SIGABRT, SIGALRM, SIGBUS, SIGFPE, SIGHUP, SIGILL, SIGINT, SIGKILL, SIGPIPE, SIGQUIT, SIGSEGV, SIGTERM, SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2 at SIG_DFL
           is terminated, and wait() reports termination by that
           signal.
note       RLIMIT_CORE is set to 0 so that the A signals make no core
           files.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/001-default-terminate.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       On XSI systems, a child that sends itself each of SIGSYS, SIGTRAP, SIGVTALRM, SIGXCPU, SIGXFSZ
           at SIG_DFL is terminated by that signal.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/002-default-terminate-xsi.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       A child that sends itself SIGRTMIN, and another SIGRTMAX, at SIG_DFL
           is terminated by that signal.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/003-default-terminate-rt.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2024
says       A process that sends itself SIGCHLD, SIGURG and SIGWINCH at SIG_DFL
           carries on unaffected (it is neither stopped nor ended).
note       SIGWINCH is new in Issue 8; SIGCHLD and SIGURG have ignored
           by default for longer. The dfl_as_catcher fault is caught
           through the sleep the child is in when the signals come.
test       t/004-default-ignore.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2001
says       A child (in the test's process group, which is not orphaned) that
           sends itself SIGSTOP, SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN or SIGTTOU at SIG_DFL is
           stopped by it (seen with WUNTRACED), does not go on while
           stopped (300 ms), and goes on after SIGCONT.
note       The group is not orphaned: the test's parent is in another
           group of the same session.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/005-default-stop.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2001
says       A caught SIGUSR1 sent to a stopped child is not handled while it is
           stopped (300 ms); SIGCONT continues it, and the handler runs
           before the child goes on from where it stopped; a SIGKILL sent
           to the child when stopped again terminates it.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/006-stopped-holds-signals.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.007
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2001
says       A child that makes itself a session leader (so its process group is
           orphaned) and sends itself SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN and SIGTTOU at SIG_DFL
           is not stopped by any of them; SIGSTOP still stops it.
note       setsid() puts the child in a new session and group; the
           parent is in another session, so the group is orphaned.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/007-orphaned-no-stop.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.008
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2024
says       With SIGCHLD, SIGURG and SIGWINCH caught, blocked and pending,
           setting each action to SIG_DFL makes it no longer pending (they
           stay blocked).
uses       XSH.sigpending.001
note       SIGWINCH is new in Issue 8.
test       t/008-dfl-discards-pending.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.009
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      1988
says       With SIGUSR1 caught, blocked and pending, setting its action to
           SIG_IGN makes it no longer pending: after a handler is installed
           again and SIGUSR1 unblocked, the handler does not run.
uses       XSH.sigpending.001
test       t/009-ign-discards-pending.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.010
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       With SIGRTMIN caught (SA_SIGINFO) and blocked, sigqueue() is called
           until it fails with EAGAIN; after SIGRTMIN is set to SIG_IGN and
           caught again, it is not pending and sigqueue() succeeds
           again.
uses       XSH.sigpending.001 XSH.sigqueue.001
timeout    120
note       When {SIGQUEUE_MAX} is indeterminate the queue is filled
           until EAGAIN, up to 1000000 (UNRESOLVED if never). The test
           briefly exhausts the queue limit, which on Linux is per user,
           so the re-queue is tried a few times in case another process
           took the freed slots.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/010-ign-releases-queue.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.011
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      1988
says       A process that ignores SIGUSR1, SIGTERM and SIGINT and sends itself
           each carries on unaffected.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/011-ignored-no-effect.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.012
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      1988
says       A loop counting to a limit, interrupted by a caught SIGALRM, goes on
           from where it was after the handler returns and reaches the
           limit, with its local state intact.
note       A weak check: almost any system passes; it guards against
           gross faults in returning from handlers.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/012-resume-after-handler.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.013
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       In a child with two threads, a SIGSTOP sent with pthread_kill() to
           the second thread stops the whole child (within 10 s): its main
           thread makes no progress until SIGCONT.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/013-thread-stop-whole-process.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.014
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       In a child with two threads, SIGUSR1 at SIG_DFL sent with
           pthread_kill() to the second thread terminates the whole child by
           SIGUSR1.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/014-thread-terminate-whole-process.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.015
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      disputed
since      2008
says       A write to a page mapped PROT_READ gives a SIGSEGV whose siginfo_t
           has si_signo SIGSEGV and si_code SEGV_ACCERR (si_addr inside that
           page is noted, not required).
note       disputed (SI-64): <signal.h> says implementations 'do not
           generate' other codes for the listed circumstances, a
           statement rather than a requirement. <signal.h> allows
           si_addr to be inaccurate, so a wrong address is only
           noted (variant sigaction.si_addr_inaccurate). The handler
           records what it saw in volatile sig_atomic_t objects and
           leaves with siglongjmp().
           On a system without XSI, sigaction() may refuse SA_SIGINFO
           for this signal (sigaction() ERRORS): EINVAL there, with
           sysconf(_SC_XOPEN_UNIX) -1, is UNRESOLVED; elsewhere it
           is a FAIL.
nomutant   decided by the kernel; a user-space shim cannot plant it
test       t/015-segv-siginfo.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.016
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       For one second a timer sends SIGALRM about every millisecond; its
           handler calls getpid(), kill(pid, 0), sigprocmask() and
           sigaction() queries, clock_gettime(), time(), open(), read(),
           fstat(), close(), write(), strlen(), memcpy() and memcmp() and
           checks each result, while the main program calls the same
           functions in a loop and checks them too; every result is right
           and the handler runs at least 100 times.
note       Calls interrupted by the handler may fail with EINTR or
           transfer less, and close() may report EINTR or EINPROGRESS:
           the sample retries or accepts those. The run lasts until the
           handler has run 100 times (30 s at most, else UNRESOLVED: no
           timer rate is promised), and times are compared on
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
nomutant   a fault would have to be specific to calls made from handlers
test       t/016-async-signal-safe.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.017
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      conditional
when       MSG
since      2008
says       With mq_notify() asking for SIGRTMIN with sigev_value 77, a message
           sent to the empty queue runs the SIGRTMIN handler (SA_SIGINFO)
           with si_code SI_MESGQ and si_value 77 (within 3 s).
nomutant   The notification comes from the message queue implementation;
           no interposed function stands between.
test       t/017-si-mesgq.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.018
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       An aio_write() of 5 bytes with SIGEV_SIGNAL, SIGRTMIN and
           sigev_value 88 runs the SIGRTMIN handler (SA_SIGINFO) on
           completion with si_code SI_ASYNCIO and si_value 88 (within
           3 s).
nomutant   The completion signal comes from the I/O implementation; no
           interposed function stands between.
test       t/018-si-asyncio.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.019
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      required
since      2008
says       When a child exits with status 7, the SIGCHLD handler (SA_SIGINFO)
           gets si_code CLD_EXITED, si_pid the child's process ID, si_uid
           the real user ID and si_status 7.
nomutant   The siginfo_t is filled by the kernel at the child's exit; no
           interposed function stands between.
note       On a system without XSI, sigaction() may refuse SA_SIGINFO
           for this signal (sigaction() ERRORS): EINVAL there, with
           sysconf(_SC_XOPEN_UNIX) -1, is UNRESOLVED; elsewhere it
           is a FAIL.
test       t/019-sigchld-info.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.020
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.3
class      disputed
since      2008
says       A read of a page unmapped just before (in the same child) gives a
           SIGSEGV whose siginfo_t has si_code SEGV_MAPERR (si_addr in that
           page is noted, not required).
note       disputed (SI-64): <signal.h> says implementations 'do not
           generate' other codes for the listed circumstances, which
           reads as a statement rather than a requirement, and XSH 2.4.3
           allows implementation-defined codes for events it does not
           list. <signal.h> allows si_addr to be inaccurate, so a wrong
           address is only noted.
           On a system without XSI (sysconf(_SC_XOPEN_UNIX) -1),
           sigaction() may refuse SA_SIGINFO for SIGSEGV: UNRESOLVED
           there, a FAIL elsewhere.
nomutant   The fault is raised by the hardware and kernel; no interposed
           function stands between.
test       t/020-segv-maperr.c

assertion  XSH.2.4.3-signal-actions.021
anchor     functions/mmap.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       Reading the second page of a two-page MAP_SHARED mapping of a
           one-page file raises SIGBUS in the reading thread (si_addr in
           that page is noted, not required).
note       The file is one page from the start: references to a part of
           a file removed after mmap() are unspecified (mmap()), while
           whole pages past the end of the object give SIGBUS.
           <signal.h> allows si_addr to be inaccurate, so a wrong
           address is only noted (Q-50).
           On a system without XSI (sysconf(_SC_XOPEN_UNIX) -1),
           sigaction() may refuse SA_SIGINFO for SIGBUS; the test then
           uses a one-argument handler and still requires the SIGBUS.
           Elsewhere the refusal is a FAIL.
nomutant   The fault is raised by the hardware and kernel; no interposed
           function stands between.
test       t/021-sigbus-beyond-end.c
